 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Multiple Intersections:** The algorithm assumes a single root within the segment defined by differing signs [S1, unofficial]. It can falsely return the last intersection point if three intersections are contained within a voxel but two of them are within the first ray segment [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Complex Topologies:** In cases where the function has two roots, the entry and exit densities may be on the same side of $\rho_{iso}$, causing the initial sign check to return NO HIT incorrectly [S1, unofficial].
-*   **SIMD Compatibility:** The control flow involves conditional updates to $t_0, t_1, \rho_0, \rho_1$ based on sign comparisons [S1, unofficial]. This inconsistent decision-making on which segment to handle makes the base algorithm less suitable for SIMD than methods with uniform control flow, although it is still functional and faster than Schwarze's method in SIMD contexts [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** As an iterative approximation, it is prone to approximation artifacts and image errors for complex voxel configurations compared to exact methods [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Multiple Intersections:** The algorithm assumes a single root within the segment defined by differing signs [S2, unofficial]. It can falsely return the last intersection point if three intersections are contained within a voxel but two of them are within the first ray segment [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Complex Topologies:** In cases where the function has two roots, the entry and exit densities may be on the same side of $\rho_{iso}$, causing the initial sign check to return NO HIT incorrectly [S2, unofficial].
+*   **SIMD Compatibility:** The control flow involves conditional updates to $t_0, t_1, \rho_0, \rho_1$ based on sign comparisons [S2, unofficial]. This inconsistent decision-making on which segment to handle makes the base algorithm less suitable for SIMD than methods with uniform control flow, although it remains functional and faster than exact solvers in parallel contexts [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Approximation Errors:** As an iterative approximation, it linearly approximates the trilinear function, making it error-prone and prone to approximation artifacts and image errors for complex voxel configurations [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
