 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Faster than the accurate but slow Schwarze algorithm [S1, unofficial]. Better suited for SIMD implementation than Schwarze due to simpler control flow (though still not ideal) [S1, unofficial]. Integrated into the OpenRT realtime ray tracing system [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Prone to approximation artifacts and image errors for complex voxel configurations [S1, unofficial]. Can fail to locate valid intersections in cases with multiple roots or specific topological configurations [S1, unofficial]. Less numerically stable than isolation-based methods [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Computationally very cheap and faster than the accurate but slow Schwarze algorithm [S1][S2, unofficial]. Better suited for SIMD implementation than Schwarze due to simpler control flow [S2, unofficial]. Integrated into the OpenRT realtime ray tracing system [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Prone to approximation artifacts and image errors for complex voxel configurations [S2, unofficial]. Can fail to locate valid intersections in cases with multiple roots or specific topological configurations [S2, unofficial]. Less numerically stable than isolation-based methods [S2, unofficial].
 
 **When to Use:**
-Use when performance is critical and the voxel data topology is relatively simple, or when an approximate intersection is acceptable [S1, unofficial]. It is a good baseline for SIMD-accelerated volume rendering where the overhead of exact solvers is prohibitive [S1, unofficial].
+Use when computational speed is prioritized over exactness and the voxel data topology is relatively simple [S1]. The method is well-suited for scenarios where an approximate intersection is acceptable and exact solvers are too slow [S2, unofficial].
 
 **When Not to Use:**
-Avoid when high accuracy is required, especially for complex iso-surfaces with multiple intersections within a single voxel, or when numerical stability is paramount [S1, unofficial].
+Avoid when high accuracy is required or when the iso-surface may contain multiple intersections within a single voxel, as the method is error-prone and may miss valid intersections [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Neubauer's algorithm was proposed by Neubauer et al. in 2002 [S1, unofficial]. It was analyzed and compared with other ray-voxel intersection techniques (including Schwarze's method and linear interpolation) in the context of iso-surface volume rendering by Wald et al. in 2004 [S1, unofficial]. The method is integrated into the OpenRT realtime ray tracing system [S1, unofficial] for iso-surface volume rendering, where it serves as a fast, approximate intersection primitive [S1, unofficial].
